r and  locate missing persons  victims of homicides and mass disasters  it can also be used to better understand  how insects are attracted to a body  throughout the course of decomposition  since this pattern can be used to help  understand times and steps  it can also be used to demonstrate the  presence of a body and there's actually  been a couple cases in the u.s and in  europe where  decomposition odor was collected as  evidence in a case  and used to show that a body was present  even after it had been moved to a  different location  there's also a lot of interest in using  some of these target molecules that we  detect in decomposition odor profiling  to develop handheld detection devices  that could be used in victim recovery  and finally it does help us to better  understand what's actually happening as  these remains are decomposing  so how the soft tissues are decomposing  eventually to the point where  remains are skeletal  and while while that's all very helpful  profiling this odor is actually very  challenging from an analytical chemistry  point of view  so i wanted to go through some of the  challenges here first  decomposition odor is a complex mixture  of hundreds if not thousands of  compounds  all mixed together these compounds also  exist in a variety of different compound  classes  and from an analytical perspective it's  difficult to  develop a method that's able to detect  all of those compound classes at the  same time  there's also a broad dynamic range  meaning that some compounds are very  high in abundance and some compounds are  very very trace in amounts  and having an analytical method that can  detect both of those concentration  ranges can be really tough  we also need to perform longitudinal  analysis meaning that we have to  understand  the odor as it evolves over time so  often times we're dealing with many many  samples because we're collecting from  the first point after death all the way  to the end of the study  also this odor is impacted by many  variables some of which we'll discuss  today  and finally we do non-targeted analysis  at this point because that's where this  field of research currently is  we care about every single compound  that's being produced by the body  and trying to understand how it changes  over time and with different variables  impacting it so since we want to know  every compound that's there that also  makes our job as analytical chemists  quite challenging because we need to  develop a method that can target  everything  so current studies have been performed  in a variety of different ways and this  area of research has really been being  developed since about 2004  the earlier studies looked at a single  point in time  mostly from bodies that were both  indoors and outdoors but  this this looked at a specific time  point  more recent studies have focused more on  looking at this decomposition process  and how it impacts odor over time  largely from bodies outdoors because  from a logistical standpoint if you're  going to be decomposing remains  having them outdoors has a lot of  benefits but in a variety of different  scenarios such as  bodies which are buried bodies which are  deposited  on the surface say on soil or on grass  remains that have been submerged and  those that have more recently in some  studies been placed under collapse  structures to simulate  mass disaster scenarios  studies have also looked at  decomposition of both human  and animal remains the animal remains  studies typically focus on susgrofo  domesticus which is the  a pig carcass and some of these studies  have been performed at tafenomic  research facilities sometimes you hear  those referred to as  body farms but these are outdoor  facilities where both  human and animal remains can be placed  to observe  and collect samples  so in collecting decomposition odor the  most common way of doing so  is by placing a large stainless steel  hood which you can see in the photo here  and you place that over top of the  remains so this hood is sitting over top  of a pig carcass  and what we do is we use a low flow  sampling pump which is essentially just  a low  uh flow vacuum and we use that to  withdraw air  through a sorbent tube this is a  stainless steel tube that's  on the inside it has a sorbent and that  basically just acts like a trap so as we  pull air through that sorbent tube  through teflon tubing and then through  the tube um our volatiles are going to  be trapped on this tube and then we can  take that back to the laboratory for  analysis  in the laboratory there are two types of  analytical instrumentation that are used  to analyze decomposition odor  the first is gas chromatography most  often with mass spectrometry  and the second is comprehensive  two-dimensional gas chromatography or  gcgc  one-dimensional gc is comprised of an  inlet  where we introduce our sample the inlet  is hot so it makes our sample into a  vapor  that vapor then gets swept onto a column  and that column is going to separate our  chemicals from one another  ideally what then happens is our  compounds will arrive at the end of the  column where a mass spectrometer will be  able to identify each of them  and tell us how much of each compound is  there and that is how we build the odor  profile  more recently studies have started to  use comprehensive two-dimensional gas  chromatography or gcgc  in this technique we have all of the  same components as one-dimensional gc  however we have two modulator and a  secondary column  what happens in this technique is as our  compounds come off  of the first column or the primary  column they reach  to the modulator which is at point  number one that modulator  slowly traps and releases small plugs  coming off of the primary column and  sends each of those plugs onto a  secondary column  it collects about two or three seconds  of effluent from the primary column  before sending that plug  onto the secondary column where it goes  through a second  independent mechanism of separation  what this means is that we are better  able to separate our compounds from one  another  because every uh part of our sample goes  through two different separations  and we still use a mass spectrometer to  detect the compounds at the end of that  process  so in gc or one-dimensional gc we have  a plot that basically shows us peaks for  each individual compound so we can see a  sample here  we've got five or six larger components  and a lot of smaller components in this  sample  with gcgc however we are able to  separate  in two dimensions because we have two  columns and if we look at our plots they  tend to look a little bit more like a  heat map where each of these individual  we could call them blobs that's actually  the term that's that's used  but each of these individual blobs would  represent an individual compound  and so we can see in this bottom plot  here there's still  a big chunk along the bottom that's not  resolved  but if we look to everything that's kind  of above that area  we can see many many components that  weren't  necessarily visible or separated in the  plot above so this is actually the same  sample analyzed by both techniques  so compared to gcms gcgcms provides us  with better  heat capacity and because we can pull  our compounds apart from one another  better  we have increased sensitivity and  detectability of our compounds  and our compounds show up in these kind  of patterns  along the plot that allow us to be able  to start identifying them more easily  this means that when we're doing  decomposition odor analysis we get more  information about the sample  we have improved visibility over  differences  from one sample to the next and  therefore we can actually just start to  focus  only on the compounds that are the most  important in our samples  because we can actually separate them  from things like background  and that's important because there are  so many variables that impact the  production of decomposition odor  we have intrinsic variables things  coming from the body or the carcass  itself  extrinsic variables like weather and  climate that are impacting the body  analytical variables like the type of  instrumentation that we're using the  type of maybe sorbent in our sorbent  tube so different analytical things  related to the collection and analysis  process  and taponomic variables like how long  has the body  actually been there um and  what scavengers are in the area etc  and we have started to study a lot of  these factors  um and so on the last slide you would  have seen asterisks  on different points where we've actually  started to study a lot of these  different  variables and from that a lot of the  studies have started to evolve and i can  say that the most effective studies have  some things in common  first off using animal models to develop  different methods is really helpful  because it allows us to have multiple  carcass replicates  this is helpful because we can start to  see how trends vary  from one body to another however of  course it's always  helpful and necessary for us to validate  those trends that we see  on human remains it's also helpful when  studies monitor  decomposition odor over more than just  a single point in time having a single  point in time  doesn't necessarily give us a full  picture  of what's actually happening  the it's also helpful when  representative control sites or  background sites are used  in replicate because this helps us to  develop better statistics  so that we can understand what's  happening in our samples and  differentiate  the background that may be coming from  things like soil or plants  or building components that maybe are in  the area  that aren't necessarily contributing to  decomposition odor but nevertheless  show up in the sample that's collected  it's also helpful to have analytical  controls for collection and analysis  so for example having blanks that help  to show  what volatiles are coming from the  stainless steel hood what volatiles are  coming from the sorbent tubes themselves  etc it's also helpful to use the  appropriate analytical tool for the  question at hand  so if a study is looking to perform  untargeted analysis  gcgc is going to be the better tool  because we have a better ability  to comprehensively separate all the  analytes in the samples  however if a study just wanted to look  at maybe five or ten volatiles and how  they are changing over time  a one-dimensional gc analysis may be  perfectly suitable  it's also helpful when studies report  significant compounds  in their samples based on statistical  variants i.e  how compounds change from one sample to  the next  not necessarily abundance if we look to  a lot of the earlier studies in the  field  researchers tended to focus largely on  the compounds that were the most  abundant and we now know  that some of the lower level compounds  are actually very important in that  profile as well  so over time we've been developing more  and more studies  in this area of research it started  around 2004 where the first study was  published  and now in 2021 there's approaching 50  published research papers specifically  on decomposition odor analysis  i have a couple asterisks here one at  2008 and one  in 2011 and that's because in 2008  there was the first occurrence of a case  where decomposition odor was collected  as evidence in the case in 2011 that  case went to trial  and that decomposition odor was  presented in court for the first time  this was at a time when there were fewer  than 10 published studies in the field  of decomposition odor  so i like to point out how far we've  actually come from that point  in exponentially increasing the amount  of research being conducted in this  field  and how much more helpful that  information could be you know to a case  that's similar to that at this point in  time  and if we look on the right hand side of  the slide we can see how the techniques  have been evolving  so the earlier on studies tended to  focus mostly on one-dimensional gc  whereas now we start to see a lot more  occurrence of comprehensive  two-dimensional gc  in these research papers  so what we know from a lot of these  studies is that  during the fresh stage which occurs  immediately after death  there are very few compounds present and  they tend to be lower in abundance  that's what we see here with low  concentration of compounds and lower  diversity in the fresh stage  once the body starts to decompose it  becomes bloated  and we get a lot higher concentration of  the compounds that are  that are being produced but still we  don't have too many compounds present  the active decay stage when there's a  large amount of insect activity and a  large amount of soft tissue loss  is when we have a peak in the  concentration of decomposition odor  as well as the diversity and the amount  of compounds we have  this is where the most compounds are  typically detected in all  studies and then in the advanced decay  stage  we start to see that those  concentrations start to diminish  because there is less soft tissue  present however we do see the  persistence of these at low levels  meaning that we maintain the diversity  of those compounds that's detected in  the profile  so again just to cover the the stages  and what's happening  during the fresh stage there are very  few vocs detected  all at low abundance in the bloat stage  that number of vocs will start to rise  but we have a lot of evolution of  concentration of those because  putrefaction is occurring  during active decay the vocs are at peak  production  because there's a lot of putrefaction  liquefaction and microbial processes  happening  and in a lot of studies we see thousands  of vocs detected at this stage  all at very high abundance the advanced  decay stage we start to see  the abundance of those vocs reduce  however the complexity of that profile  becomes maintained  and during the dry or remains or  skeleton stage  we reduce those number of compounds and  really the  the compounds that will be retained will  be the ones that are in the environment  that are the most persistent  so what we know about decomposition odor  right now is that we have at least  hundreds of compounds in about 10  different chemical classes  that this profile is dominated by sulfur  compounds  namely dimethyl sulfide dimethyl  disulfide  dimethyl trisulfide this odor starts  minimal  it will peak in abundance and complexity  and then it will reduce  and it's impacted by many factors  including temperature and humidity  and insect activity as probably being  the three main environmental factors  the the odor that we collect will be  slightly biased by our collection  methods so if we use different methods  of collecting this odor then we may get  slightly different profiles  however there is a subset of this  profile that is reproducible under  different conditions  in totally different environments at  totally different times of the year  we also know that live odor transitions  to postmodern or  post mortem odor after about 43 hours or  about two days postmortem  what we still don't know in this field  is  the mechanisms behind how a specific  compound is produced  so we can't necessarily track a starting  point to an  ending point in terms of which compounds  are being released  and we also don't know how to predict  the presence in the specific amount of a  compound at a very specific point in  time  meaning that we don't necessarily know  if we could use  certain compounds as markers of  post-mortem interval  we also still don't fully understand the  impact of microbial communities on this  odor and  we know that microbial communities have  a very big impact on how a body will  decompose  so that's still a big blind spot in this  field of research  and because we still have some of these  blind spots we don't necessarily know  how to interpret samples  if they are collected in casework so it  would be best for us to develop a better  understanding of this before again  presenting this type of research in  court  looking to a map these are some of the  areas in which voc studies on  decomposition odor have been conducted  if we look at climate classifications we  can see that the large majority of these  studies fall into the common class c  and d the area that i'm currently  located in  is actually common class a in honolulu  hawaii where we're actually designated  in the tropical rainforest category  so our most recent study focused on  looking at decomposition odor of pig  carcasses in this region  and seeing how that can compare to other  regions that have been studied around  the world  this is our field site in palolo valley  which is in honolulu hawaii  and we can see an aerial view on the  right-hand side  of you kind of looking out towards what  we call diamond head  if you've ever been to hawaii you  probably recognize it it's very close to  waikiki  and we also have a photo of some  researchers  outside at our field site taking some  measurements from a pig carcass  so this is a type of terrain that we are  typically looking at it's  very similar to a lot of the areas that  people go missing especially  missing hikers missing elderly people  etc  so it's very representative of you know  the scenario that i  i started this presentation with and in  studying this we monitor decomposition  odor over a two week period  which is typically about enough time in  hawaii for our  remains to go all the way to a skeleton  state or at least to advance decay  in this we were able to detect all major  compound classes that we know exist in  decomposition odor  except aromatics and alkanes the acids  sulfur compounds esters and nitrogen  compounds were the  most significant components of the  profile  the temporal trends or the trends over  time appear to exist for most major  classes  in very similar ways to areas such as  greece  canada and australia and obviously if we  want to compare  our study to other studies we can only  compare it to studies that have  similar study designs that monitor over  a similar  period of time etc and therefore  um you know this list is really only  repres this list of places that are  similar is really only representative of  studies  that have done similar types of research  or design their study in a similar way  if we look at these vocs by  decomposition stage we can kind of see  them separated  and we see how the vocs are lower in  abundance in the beginning and then they  kind of peak  and then eventually start to decrease  and  the sulfur compounds did start to  dominate that active decay stage which  is very similar to other studies  we see that the acids started to peak in  later decomposition again  these trends have high consistently with  similar data that are reported in  belgium and australia and so this is  very encouraging to see  that in a totally different climate we  can identify  new trends or sorry similar trends in in  new regions that are  you know consistent from one place to  another  and of course a lot of our research has  been impacted by the kovid 19 pandemic  um i wanted to give a bit of an idea of  how this kind of impacted our work  for those who don't know chaminade  university is a predominantly  undergraduate institution we're located  in honolulu on the island of oahu which  is what people probably considered the  you know the main  island in hawaii um and  uh we've been you know rather isolated  by  by an ocean border um throughout this  pandemic and so  that's been a benefit in some ways and  in other ways  kind of a barrier at least to our  research um  but in march 2020 we lost full access to  the university about  um two weeks into march uh was when that  first happened you know  very similar to i imagine what happened  around the entire world about that time  for us we were quite lucky that we  actually regained access for research  purposes  only to the university in may of 2020  and this was because an exemption was  uh was put in place in our locale  for research to continue and so the  university granted us  access however we had to go through  quite a bit of paperwork for every  researcher coming back on campus  we were only allowed to have limited  personnel in our space absolutely it  makes sense  because of what was going on we also had  to have  additional safety protocols in place  that obviously is necessary but also  impacts your productivity  and we had to train everybody on these  new protocols  and as well we were also training new  researchers in the laboratory on new  techniques and so  that makes it really challenging when  you're trying to train new people in the  laboratory but you have restrictions on  the the number of people you're allowed  to have in that space so it just changes  a little bit the way that  you know you have to work in august of  2020  um we actually started our fall semester  and at chaminade university  that was a fully online start to the  semester and so we did about six weeks  of online studying and then in october  2020 all of our classes resumed  face to face and we've been face to face  ever since with the option for students  to study online if they want to  and i point this out um because at  predominantly undergraduate institutions  or puis  a lot of our research space is actually  located  in classrooms and so that's a huge  consideration because through the summer  months  we were basically able to maintain  social distance and make sure that we  had limited people in those spaces  but as soon as classes resumed it became  a lot more challenging to book time in a  laboratory  to access the instrumentation to access  our data workstations and things like  that  so even though you know classes and  research at a lot of institutions are  taken completely  separately for us the start of classes  even online and face-to-face  actually did have quite an impact on our  ability to get in there and do work  from a field standpoint if we were to be  conducting field research  throughout this time of course impact to  field facilities could be challenging  so first off you have to make sure that  your personnel get access to your field  facilities  typically that involves preparing in a  laboratory environment  before going out to those field sites  you're talking about teams of  researchers needing to access those  facilities  so you know you have to think about  people driving together in a car  to that location by the institution  it may not be um there also may be an  impact of  impact of availability of research  specimens so for example  we get pig carcasses from a local pig  farmer  and so that can be challenging if  they're not willing to provide things  throughout you know these major  incidents in terms of funding if you're  working on an existing funding program  you really have to think about how are  you  going to make your funding program work  given a totally new timeline  granting agencies of course were very  understanding of setbacks to research  over the past year  however if you're working on you know a  multi-year  funding program um keeping  researchers employed and keeping  equipment running and maintenance going  and everything all of that is going to  be  impacted in the long term in how much  you can really achieve  throughout your funding program and also  this area of research is  an area where there's already very  minimal funding and there's been a huge  uptick in the number of  grant proposals that have been submitted  across i think  probably all areas of science right now  as people were  you know at home writing and compiling  you know previous data and things like  that throughout the pandemic and so  that funding availability could also be  an impact  you know moving forward at least in the  next year or two  in the laboratory a couple things that  we have to think about as well  obviously i've already kind of talked a  little bit about personal access to  laboratory  um but i also wanted to mention for us  one of the things that was  really challenging and completely  understandable from my perspective  was the level of commitment that my  researchers were able to put towards  their projects  my entire research group is  undergraduate researchers a lot of them  have part-time jobs  they're also studying full-time and they  have family commitments outside of the  university  and a lot of that really impacted the  ability for us to have really high  expectations  of how much could be performed in a  certain period of time  so we had to adjust a little bit the  expectations about  the commitment to you know performing  research hours because these are  part-time workers  we also had to think a little bit about  if we have team projects and multiple  people working on things how do we  organize to have them  in the laboratory at the right time how  do we make our workflow in terms of data  processing and all of that work properly  we had to think about how do we take  data that's on our  our computers here at the university  and how do we make that accessible for  researchers when they're working  remotely and things like that  the equipment also had to keep running  throughout this entire time so we needed  access to techno  or we we actually had limited access to  technicians we had two incidents where  we were supposed to have technicians  come out to do you know regular  maintenance  um and those things were were definitely  impeded as well  and being for us at least that we're an  island in the middle of the pacific  ocean  obviously we had challenges with  shipping of supplies  and we also have to think about how  we're using our funding for example  because um as we have to keep our  instruments running with helium  constantly which is very expensive  if we're not actually running samples  we're still actually funding the  the use of that instrumentation even  while it's sitting idle so  just some things that we had to kind of  think about and plan for at least as we  move into  an area of time where we hope things are  going to start to return a little bit  more back to normal  so in conclusion about this work i would  say that the field of decomposition odor  is still  relatively young it's developing but  it's getting there and we are  learning so much more through every  study about what this odor is  what the profiles look like and how that  can be applied in different scenarios  so we've learned a great deal about  these trends and how stable they are in  different areas  we still need some validation studies  some is probably an understatement i  would say a lot more validation studies  are needed to really put this into  practice  better modeling of this data that we've  even already collected to look at trends  maybe looking at using some more  advanced statistical models to  try to pinpoint how this odor is  impacted over time and maybe even use it  as a post-mortem interval  marker and also i talked a lot about the  different things that make a study  the most effective and i think  implementing those across a lot of  studies will be more helpful  for studies in the future and of course  cova 19 will have an impact on most  research groups to some extent either in  the laboratory or  the field or in both capacities and so  we have to also kind of understand  uh what we're up against moving forward  and hopefully you know  moving more into a time that's more  normal  and i just wanted to take a moment to  acknowledge all of the researchers in my  group  they are very hardworking we've all been  through a lot this past year  and it always amazes me every day that  they  continue to work so hard on their  research projects so i just want to  take a moment to thank everybody and  also to thank our  um to thank the organizers of this  conference  it's a pleasure to be able to be  involved um all of our funding agencies  and of course if you have any questions  you're more than welcome to reach out to  me  and i've just attached my email to the  bottom of this slide  thank you very much
 

